---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeOvenTemp from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeOvenTemp.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="oven-temperature-converter">
  <UnitsLifeOvenTemp client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Four scales for one dial</h2>
    <p>
      An oven temperature has four forms, and a recipe uses the one its author knows: degrees
      Fahrenheit in the US, degrees Celsius almost everywhere else, gas marks in Britain and Ireland,
      and a fan or convection setting, about 20 &deg;C lower than the conventional number for the same
      result.
    </p>
    <p>
      This oven temperature converter handles all four, including gas marks and fan settings, which
      cause most ruined dinners.
    </p>

    <h2>The fan oven rule</h2>
    <p>
      A fan (convection) oven moves the air, which removes the insulating boundary layer that stays
      against food in still air. So heat transfers faster at the same air temperature, and the standard
      correction is to <strong>reduce the setting by 20 &deg;C</strong>, or about 25&ndash;30 &deg;F.
    </p>
    <p>
      So 180 &deg;C conventional is 160 &deg;C fan. 200 &deg;C conventional is 180 &deg;C fan. If a
      recipe says "180 &deg;C (160 &deg;C fan)", it has already done this. If it gives one number and
      you have a fan oven, subtract.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cooking time also drops, usually by about 10%, and most for food with an exposed surface. Fan is
      excellent for roasting and batch baking, but can be too strong for delicate custards, soufflés
      and rich fruit cakes, so many ovens let you switch it off.
    </p>

    <h2>Gas marks</h2>
    <p>
      Above mark 1 the scale follows a clean formula: <strong>&deg;F = 25 &times; (mark + 10)</strong>.
      Mark 4 is 25 &times; 14 = 350 &deg;F. Mark 7 is 425 &deg;F. Below mark 1 the pattern breaks:
      mark 1/2 is 250 &deg;F and mark 1/4 is 225 &deg;F, because those settings were added later for
      slow cooking and meringues.
    </p>
    <p>
      Gas ovens also have a stronger vertical temperature gradient than electric ones: the top shelf
      can be a full gas mark hotter than the bottom. So British recipes often specify a shelf position.
    </p>

    <h2>The descriptive terms</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">Conventional &deg;C</th><th scope="col">&deg;F</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Very cool / very slow</td><td>110&ndash;120</td><td>225&ndash;250</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cool / slow</td><td>140&ndash;150</td><td>275&ndash;300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warm</td><td>170</td><td>325</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moderate</td><td>180</td><td>350</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moderately hot</td><td>190&ndash;200</td><td>375&ndash;400</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hot</td><td>220&ndash;230</td><td>425&ndash;450</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Very hot</td><td>240&ndash;260</td><td>475&ndash;500</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Recipes written before ovens had thermostats used only these terms, and they still appear in
      reprints of older cookbooks.
    </p>

    <h2>Your oven dial is probably wrong</h2>
    <p>
      Domestic ovens often run 10&ndash;20 &deg;C away from their dial, and the thermostat switches the
      element on and off, so the air temperature moves around the set point. An oven thermometer costs
      a few pounds and tells you more about your baking than any conversion chart, including which
      shelf is the hot one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Preheat fully. When the light goes out, the air is at temperature, but the walls and the shelves
      are not, and a pizza stone can take forty minutes to heat up.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
